From f301bc7958592cc3ae76a4b0a7b6e6194f3cbcb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VideoPlayerGold <127964164+FlyWheel998@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 13:37:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] vf_nvvfx: NVIDIA Video Effects denoise/de-blocking as a
 GPU-resident mpv filter

Runs the SDK effect on the decoder's own CUDA frames, removing the 57.7 ms host
round trip that made the same effect unusable at 4K through VapourSynth (the
effect itself was only ~13 ms of the 70.9 ms measured). Built on vf_cudapass,
whose plumbing measured 0.52 ms/frame.

Differences from the zhangmq/vsr-player design this follows:
 - limited-range conversion divides by 219/224, not 239 for both; the reference
   feeds the network a picture ~8.4% too dark and ~6.3% desaturated
 - the requested level actually reaches the SDK (the reference always sent its
   upscale quality, so its denoise setting did nothing)
 - YUV kernels are precompiled to PTX and embedded, not NVRTC-compiled at every
   start: no ~97 MB runtime dependency, no CUDA toolkit in the cross-build
 - output is YUV in the input's own format, so frames take the same hwdec_cuda
   path decoder frames already use

Kernels verified against an independent reference before embedding: all four
format/matrix combinations within rounding, limited-range black->0, white->255,
neutral grey stays neutral.

HDR is refused rather than degraded - the SDK carries ~9 effective bits, which
is invisible in 8-bit SDR and not invisible in 10-bit PQ.
